1
00:00:07,465 --> 00:00:08,508
Hey, Cho.

2
00:00:09,383 --> 00:00:10,551
Okay, how'd you do that?

3
00:00:10,718 --> 00:00:12,929
Well, you tread
a little heavier than Lisbon

4
00:00:13,054 --> 00:00:15,097
and Rigby's car isn't in the lot.

5
00:00:15,223 --> 00:00:17,850
We've got a case and Lisbon's busy.
So you and I are riding out.

6
00:00:17,975 --> 00:00:20,561
- Excellent. I'll meet you downstairs.
- Okay.

7
00:00:52,885 --> 00:00:54,262
Quaint.

8
00:00:54,512 --> 00:00:55,763
Yeah.

9
00:01:05,898 --> 00:01:07,650
I'm Kimball Cho.
This is Patrick Jane.

10
00:01:07,817 --> 00:01:10,528
County Coroner, Deb Granger.
Pleased to meet you.

11
00:01:10,653 --> 00:01:12,071
We don't get to work a lot
of murders around here.

12
00:01:12,238 --> 00:01:13,990
You got a little, uh,
something on your...

13
00:01:14,115 --> 00:01:16,450
Oh. Brain matter. Good catch.

14
00:01:16,576 --> 00:01:19,370
Victim's name is Hollis Percy.
61 years of age.

15
00:01:19,495 --> 00:01:20,830
His wife found him this morning.

16
00:01:20,955 --> 00:01:22,832
- And he owned the town of Percy.
- The whole town?

17
00:01:22,957 --> 00:01:24,500
Yeah. Schools, roads, bakery...

18
00:01:24,667 --> 00:01:25,876
been in his family
since the gold rush.

19
00:01:26,002 --> 00:01:28,129
Not exactly booming
by the looks of it.

20
00:01:28,254 --> 00:01:30,131
They still get a little tourism
off the highway.

21
00:01:34,885 --> 00:01:36,262
Smells of juniper.

22
00:01:36,762 --> 00:01:37,888
Cologne?

23
00:01:38,222 --> 00:01:41,642
Uh, I don't... no.
More like...

24
00:01:42,935 --> 00:01:45,688
Cheap gin...
drunken out of necessity, not pleasure.

25
00:01:45,813 --> 00:01:49,066
So, he drank alone.
He let the killer in.

26
00:01:49,191 --> 00:01:51,027
He knew him or her.

27
00:01:51,152 --> 00:01:53,404
- They argued...
- How'd you know they argued?

28
00:01:53,988 --> 00:01:56,157
- Well, he is dead.
- What did they argue about?

29
00:01:56,282 --> 00:01:57,742
Well, I don't know.
What do people argue about?

30
00:01:57,867 --> 00:02:00,536
- Money, power, love, jealousy...
- Revenge...

31
00:02:01,037 --> 00:02:03,289
- Sorry, I shouldn't do that.
- Oh, no. Jump right in.

32
00:02:03,414 --> 00:02:04,624
This is a murder. It's exciting.

33
00:02:04,749 --> 00:02:06,375
What's your guess
on time of death?

34
00:02:07,126 --> 00:02:09,128
Rigor stiffness suggests
12 hours or more.

35
00:02:09,253 --> 00:02:10,463
Probably that's your murder weapon.

36
00:02:10,588 --> 00:02:13,382
It's an antique.
Colt 1851 Navy. .36 caliber.

37
00:02:13,507 --> 00:02:14,759
Probably hasn't been fired
in a century.

38
00:02:14,884 --> 00:02:18,262
Right. So, instead, your guy...
or gal...

39
00:02:18,387 --> 00:02:19,430
- Whack.
- Whack.

40
00:02:19,555 --> 00:02:21,557
Penetrating trauma
to the back of the skull

41
00:02:21,682 --> 00:02:23,392
was introduced
by the butt of the gun.

42
00:02:23,517 --> 00:02:25,853
Once I pop the calvarium, I'll know
if that's our official cause of death.

43
00:02:25,978 --> 00:02:27,730
That's great.
Let's pop the calvarium.

44
00:02:28,564 --> 00:02:30,232
- You two hold here.
- What is...

45
00:02:32,526 --> 00:02:34,195
- We're gonna hold here.
- Oh, okay.

46
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:36,030
- They've got this covered.
- Yeah.

47
00:02:36,697 --> 00:02:37,782
"Listen up!"

48
00:02:50,961 --> 00:02:53,047
Like I said, folks, anything
can happen here

49
00:02:53,172 --> 00:02:55,549
in rough 'n' tumble
Percy, California.

50
00:02:56,008 --> 00:02:58,135
Let's... Wait... let's have uh... uh,
a big round of applause

51
00:02:58,260 --> 00:03:01,389
for our modern-day
gunslingers here, yeah.

52
00:03:01,514 --> 00:03:03,140
What the hell are you doing?
This is a crime scene.

53
00:03:03,265 --> 00:03:04,433
Don't you know
there's a dead body in there?

54
00:03:04,558 --> 00:03:06,644
These people paid 25 bucks
each in advance.

55
00:03:06,769 --> 00:03:08,229
- They're entitled to a show.
- Well, they got one.

56
00:03:08,354 --> 00:03:10,523
Now shut this down
and clear the area right now.

57
00:03:25,327 --> 00:03:26,412
"I don't know."

58
00:03:26,829 --> 00:03:29,248
I think that's why I liked
San Francisco.

59
00:03:29,373 --> 00:03:32,543
I never saw myself living
in the place I grew up.

60
00:03:32,710 --> 00:03:33,877
You know?

61
00:03:34,044 --> 00:03:35,587
Yeah. I know.

62
00:03:41,218 --> 00:03:42,344
What about you?

63
00:03:42,511 --> 00:03:43,804
Where did you grow up?

64
00:03:44,012 --> 00:03:45,806
Oh, we moved around...

65
00:03:46,765 --> 00:03:48,934
for Dad's work,
and then for Mom's work...

66
00:03:50,978 --> 00:03:54,940
{\an8}I like to say I grew up in America.

67
00:03:55,274 --> 00:03:57,234
{\an8}Yeah. Yeah.

68
00:03:57,985 --> 00:03:59,236
{\an8}Um...

69
00:04:00,404 --> 00:04:02,656
{\an8}I hate to do this,
but I have to run.

70
00:04:02,781 --> 00:04:04,074
{\an8}We have a fresh case.

71
00:04:04,199 --> 00:04:05,909
{\an8}Please. I'm glad we were able
to do this...

72
00:04:06,034 --> 00:04:07,327
{\an8}sit down like this.

73
00:04:08,537 --> 00:04:09,872
{\an8}This is nice.

74
00:04:10,205 --> 00:04:12,583
{\an8}You're really easy
to talk to, Teresa.

75
00:04:12,833 --> 00:04:14,084
Thanks, Bob.

76
00:04:15,961 --> 00:04:18,630
{\an8}Are there any new lines
of inquiry on Red John?

77
00:04:18,756 --> 00:04:20,382
We've had some leads, but...

78
00:04:20,966 --> 00:04:24,553
{\an8}I hate to admit it...
we're kinda stuck.

79
00:04:24,678 --> 00:04:26,054
Nothing. No.

80
00:04:26,221 --> 00:04:29,224
{\an8}- What about Jane?
- I don't think he has anything.

81
00:04:29,349 --> 00:04:30,809
{\an8}But he'd tell you if he did.

82
00:04:30,976 --> 00:04:33,187
Yeah. Yeah, he would.

83
00:04:33,979 --> 00:04:35,647
Okay. Well, I'm sure we'll talk.

84
00:04:35,814 --> 00:04:37,733
Homeland Security beckons.

85
00:04:46,784 --> 00:04:48,827
- Is it cold in here?
- Yeah. AC's on the fritz,

86
00:04:48,952 --> 00:04:50,579
It's stuck on cold.
They got someone coming in.

87
00:04:50,746 --> 00:04:53,373
{\an8}- So I'm not crazy, okay.
- Right.

88
00:04:53,499 --> 00:04:55,834
{\an8}Percy, California.
Incorporated by J.D. Percy

89
00:04:55,959 --> 00:04:57,753
as a trading post in 1870.

90
00:04:57,920 --> 00:04:59,588
Trade dried up,
but the Percy family stayed.

91
00:04:59,713 --> 00:05:00,756
{\an8}They tried to make it work.

92
00:05:00,881 --> 00:05:02,299
{\an8}It's basically been a tourist town
for the past 30 years.

93
00:05:02,424 --> 00:05:04,009
{\an8}But that hasn't been
going too great.

94
00:05:04,176 --> 00:05:06,261
{\an8}Sorry. It was
a "welcome back" gift.

95
00:05:06,428 --> 00:05:07,596
Very thoughtful, Wayne.

96
00:05:07,721 --> 00:05:10,766
- Well, how did you know it was from me?
- I'm a homicide detective.

97
00:05:10,933 --> 00:05:12,476
{\an8}Now, Hollis Percy owned
every building.

98
00:05:12,601 --> 00:05:13,936
{\an8}People pay him rent,
and he was the bank.

99
00:05:14,061 --> 00:05:16,855
{\an8}That property belongs to his wife,
Joanna, for a few weeks, anyway.

100
00:05:16,980 --> 00:05:18,899
{\an8}- Well, why only a few weeks?
- He ran outta money.

101
00:05:19,024 --> 00:05:20,400
{\an8}The Wild West Show
was a last-ditch chance

102
00:05:20,526 --> 00:05:22,903
{\an8}to drum up some cash, but now
the whole place is up for sale.

103
00:05:23,028 --> 00:05:25,447
{\an8}- They started selling property this year.
- Houses, stores.

104
00:05:25,572 --> 00:05:27,366
People rented for generations
and now becoming owners.

105
00:05:27,491 --> 00:05:29,493
{\an8}- A lotta money changing hands.
- There could be motive there.

106
00:05:29,618 --> 00:05:31,245
{\an8}Go through all
the real estate transactions...

107
00:05:31,370 --> 00:05:32,621
{\an8}see if anything shakes loose.

108
00:05:32,746 --> 00:05:34,832
- Any usable prints on the gun?
- Doesn't look that way.

109
00:05:34,957 --> 00:05:36,416
Okay. I'm gonna go talk
to the family.

110
00:05:36,542 --> 00:05:38,669
Make sure they take care
of this AC today.

111
00:05:38,836 --> 00:05:41,171
{\an8}- That was it?
- That's all Kirkland said.

112
00:05:41,296 --> 00:05:42,548
{\an8}He was just fishing.

113
00:05:42,798 --> 00:05:44,132
{\an8}Don't you think it's odd...

114
00:05:44,258 --> 00:05:47,469
{\an8}Homeland Security's fascination
with a California serial killer?

115
00:05:47,594 --> 00:05:49,847
{\an8}Everything about
Bob Kirkland is odd.

116
00:05:49,972 --> 00:05:51,473
{\an8}- Well, yes.
- Just add that to the list.

117
00:05:51,640 --> 00:05:52,683
{\an8}Hello there.

118
00:05:53,433 --> 00:05:55,477
Emmett Cook, chief of police.

119
00:05:55,602 --> 00:05:57,062
{\an8}Sorry I couldn't be
at the crime scene.

120
00:05:57,229 --> 00:05:58,438
{\an8}Yeah, why couldn't you be there?

121
00:05:58,605 --> 00:05:59,982
Something a little more pressing
than a murder?

122
00:06:00,107 --> 00:06:02,693
{\an8}I'm Agent Lisbon,
and this is Patrick Jane.

123
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:05,070
- Hi.
- Hi.

124
00:06:05,237 --> 00:06:08,198
{\an8}Um, actually, I couldn't be there
'cause I was dealing with a fire.

125
00:06:08,949 --> 00:06:11,034
{\an8}Quite a mess, uh,
third one this year.

126
00:06:11,159 --> 00:06:14,413
{\an8}These, uh, meth labs guys
are setting up in empty cabins, uh...

127
00:06:14,913 --> 00:06:16,582
{\an8}the things go...

128
00:06:17,416 --> 00:06:18,500
Kablooey.

129
00:06:19,126 --> 00:06:21,545
Kablooey, indeed.
So you've been busy, Chief?

130
00:06:21,670 --> 00:06:23,505
{\an8}Do you think the drug trade
had something to do

131
00:06:23,630 --> 00:06:25,215
{\an8}with Hollis Percy's murder?

132
00:06:25,340 --> 00:06:27,134
I don't see the connection. No.

133
00:06:27,426 --> 00:06:29,928
I mean, there could be,
but that would be...

134
00:06:30,053 --> 00:06:31,430
Uh, messy?

135
00:06:32,389 --> 00:06:35,350
- Yes... You could say that.
- I did.

136
00:06:36,643 --> 00:06:38,979
Well, I just wanted
to stop by and let you know

137
00:06:39,104 --> 00:06:41,481
that I am available
if CBI needs my help.

138
00:06:41,607 --> 00:06:45,527
Thank you, but that's kind
of a conflict of interest.

139
00:06:45,652 --> 00:06:47,696
You aiding in the investigation
of the murder

140
00:06:47,821 --> 00:06:49,197
of the man
who pays your salary.

141
00:06:49,364 --> 00:06:51,909
Well, plus you've got some
meth dealers to track down.

142
00:06:52,034 --> 00:06:53,869
Yes. Yes, I do.

143
00:06:54,661 --> 00:06:57,789
Well, if you need anything,
just, um...

144
00:06:58,498 --> 00:06:59,708
shout.

145
00:06:59,875 --> 00:07:01,043
We'll holler.

146
00:07:03,337 --> 00:07:05,213
Very odd little
country welcome.

147
00:07:05,380 --> 00:07:07,799
- You've had no issues, Mrs. Percy?
- No.

148
00:07:09,176 --> 00:07:10,802
There's been no trouble.

149
00:07:11,553 --> 00:07:15,265
The last murder
in Percy was a long time ago.

150
00:07:15,432 --> 00:07:17,726
It's safe here.
We have good schools.

151
00:07:17,935 --> 00:07:20,896
We... we build riding trails and...

152
00:07:21,063 --> 00:07:23,690
and Hollis maintained it
all beautifully.

153
00:07:23,857 --> 00:07:25,317
Dad loved this town.

154
00:07:25,651 --> 00:07:28,654
- But you don't... Ian?
- Of course he does.

155
00:07:28,987 --> 00:07:30,948
Yeah. I mean, you know?

156
00:07:31,114 --> 00:07:33,241
- Well, it's not a trick question.
- No, of course.

157
00:07:33,825 --> 00:07:35,827
I just moved back
to help with the transition.

158
00:07:36,453 --> 00:07:38,622
I was in San Francisco
working venture capital.

159
00:07:38,747 --> 00:07:40,290
It was time to come home.

160
00:07:40,415 --> 00:07:42,751
Was Hollis meeting someone
in his office last night?

161
00:07:42,876 --> 00:07:44,962
We think the attack took place
around 8:00.

162
00:07:45,087 --> 00:07:46,213
Well, it's possible.

163
00:07:46,380 --> 00:07:48,674
He kept a lotta late hours
the past few years.

164
00:07:48,799 --> 00:07:50,217
Yeah, he tried everything...

165
00:07:50,759 --> 00:07:52,636
talking to investors, developers

166
00:07:52,761 --> 00:07:54,930
but, you know,
towns like this are just dying out.

167
00:07:55,514 --> 00:08:00,936
Still, he was a feudal lord ruling over
a town of downtrodden serfs.

168
00:08:01,103 --> 00:08:02,604
He must have had someone
that hated him.

169
00:08:02,729 --> 00:08:05,524
He was respected.
Ask anyone.

170
00:08:05,649 --> 00:08:07,484
What about the sale
of the town?

171
00:08:07,609 --> 00:08:08,902
Was there any friction there?

172
00:08:11,113 --> 00:08:12,823
So he had a few critics.

173
00:08:13,031 --> 00:08:14,324
Could you do me a favor?

174
00:08:14,449 --> 00:08:16,118
Just grab a piece of paper
there and a pen

175
00:08:16,243 --> 00:08:19,162
and write down the name
that you just thought of, just now.

176
00:08:19,287 --> 00:08:21,248
Write it down.
Don't show it to each other.

177
00:08:21,373 --> 00:08:22,791
- Why?
- Please?

178
00:08:34,469 --> 00:08:36,263
Francisco Navarro.

179
00:08:37,681 --> 00:08:38,890
And you had the same thought.

180
00:08:39,057 --> 00:08:41,852
- Who is Francisco Navarro?
- He runs a Wild West Show.

181
00:08:42,019 --> 00:08:43,478
He's an old friend
of the family...

182
00:08:44,229 --> 00:08:46,189
and he didn't think
we should sell.

183
00:08:46,356 --> 00:08:48,066
Hollis did what he had to do.

184
00:08:48,692 --> 00:08:51,778
A bunch of amateurs
playing cowboys and Indians

185
00:08:51,903 --> 00:08:55,240
weren't gonna save us no matter
what Francisco believed.

186
00:08:55,365 --> 00:08:57,075
He's pretty dedicated to this place.

187
00:08:57,242 --> 00:08:59,244
You think he was angry enough
to act on it?

188
00:08:59,411 --> 00:09:02,039
Like I said... He's dedicated.

189
00:09:04,041 --> 00:09:05,500
That's a nice jacket, Mr. Navarro.

190
00:09:08,128 --> 00:09:09,379
What would you call that...

191
00:09:09,921 --> 00:09:11,048
a duster?

192
00:09:11,173 --> 00:09:13,550
I'm the medicine man in the show.
I'm going right there from here.

193
00:09:13,675 --> 00:09:15,052
Just answer the question.

194
00:09:15,218 --> 00:09:16,636
Yes. It's a duster.

195
00:09:18,805 --> 00:09:21,475
- Are you writing that down?
- Tell me about the Wild West Show.

196
00:09:21,683 --> 00:09:24,519
Our program is a fully immersive
step back in the 19th century.

197
00:09:24,686 --> 00:09:26,146
I oversee 20 performers.

198
00:09:26,271 --> 00:09:27,939
We run six days a week.
Wednesdays and Fridays

199
00:09:28,065 --> 00:09:29,816
we stage a medicine man show.

200
00:09:29,941 --> 00:09:31,234
"And you're in charge?"

201
00:09:31,902 --> 00:09:33,945
"Which makes this piece
on your blog..."

202
00:09:34,112 --> 00:09:35,363
kind of odd.

203
00:09:36,782 --> 00:09:39,159
- "Killing Percy."
- Well, I meant the town of Percy

204
00:09:39,326 --> 00:09:42,037
- not Hollis Percy, obviously.
- Yeah. That's some bad timing.

205
00:09:42,162 --> 00:09:44,956
Yes, it is.
It is also the inalienable right

206
00:09:45,082 --> 00:09:46,333
of every citizen
in the state of California

207
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:48,293
to freely speak, write,
and publish his sentiments

208
00:09:48,418 --> 00:09:51,671
on all subjects. I overreacted...
online.

209
00:09:52,130 --> 00:09:53,632
I would never do harm
to my friend.

210
00:09:53,757 --> 00:09:56,134
- But he wasn't your friend.
- No, he was.

211
00:09:59,179 --> 00:10:02,516
But... when he started
selling property last year

212
00:10:03,850 --> 00:10:06,770
See, that's just giving up on something
worth fighting for. He was weak.

213
00:10:06,937 --> 00:10:08,313
And he couldn't afford
to run the town?

214
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:09,773
Which meant your show
was gonna get shut down.

215
00:10:09,898 --> 00:10:11,024
Yeah, and what thanks do I get?

216
00:10:11,191 --> 00:10:12,984
I built our program
to save Percy.

217
00:10:13,110 --> 00:10:14,778
Tourists came...
just not enough.

218
00:10:14,903 --> 00:10:16,947
And then with these cabins
exploding up there in the woods

219
00:10:17,072 --> 00:10:18,115
"it's getting ugly."

220
00:10:18,240 --> 00:10:19,282
Where were you last night
at 8:00 p.m.?

221
00:10:19,407 --> 00:10:21,118
I was in the bar
with some of my actors.

222
00:10:21,243 --> 00:10:22,661
If you're so dialed into the town

223
00:10:22,786 --> 00:10:23,912
who else had a beef
with your friend?

224
00:10:24,037 --> 00:10:26,915
I have no idea. Look, I gotta go.
I have a show to put on.

225
00:10:27,082 --> 00:10:29,167
"His alibi relies on the account
of a bunch of drunks."

226
00:10:29,292 --> 00:10:32,045
Real drunks
or old-timey pioneer drunks

227
00:10:32,212 --> 00:10:33,505
that are getting paid
by the hour?

228
00:10:33,630 --> 00:10:35,382
Well, either way, he could have left
the bar at any point

229
00:10:35,507 --> 00:10:37,884
walked 30 yards
to Hollis' office, and walked out.

230
00:10:38,176 --> 00:10:39,761
Oh, we're heading
to the bar now.

231
00:10:40,053 --> 00:10:41,179
All right. We're gonna go meet
the police chief.

232
00:10:41,304 --> 00:10:43,098
And then Rigsby wants to go
check out those cabins.

233
00:10:43,265 --> 00:10:46,476
The meth lab fires? Might be worth
looking into, I guess.

234
00:10:46,643 --> 00:10:50,272
Follow close as my magical and mysterious
friend Mr. Golden Nugget

235
00:10:50,438 --> 00:10:53,358
travels a trail of deception
before your very eyes.

236
00:10:53,483 --> 00:10:55,652
Young man, under which
of these humble chalices

237
00:10:55,777 --> 00:10:58,363
does the golden nugget reside?

238
00:10:59,239 --> 00:11:00,407
That one.

239
00:11:01,283 --> 00:11:04,494
A brave choice
but sadly, the wrong one.

240
00:11:05,036 --> 00:11:07,747
Hey! Care to wager again?
A buck buys three guesses

241
00:11:07,873 --> 00:11:09,541
but pays 5
if you guess right.

242
00:11:09,708 --> 00:11:11,501
I-I believe
I-I saw your move.

243
00:11:11,751 --> 00:11:14,880
Oh, what have we here...
A skeptic?

244
00:11:15,005 --> 00:11:17,757
Please sir, step right up.
Test my skills.

245
00:11:18,008 --> 00:11:20,260
See if you remain unconvinced.

246
00:11:20,385 --> 00:11:21,887
- Don't mind if I do. Okay...
- All right.

247
00:11:22,012 --> 00:11:23,346
So the nugget goes
in the cup like this.

248
00:11:23,471 --> 00:11:24,598
And then we move them
around like this

249
00:11:24,723 --> 00:11:26,141
until I can't tell
which one it's in.

250
00:11:26,266 --> 00:11:28,393
Then you continue doing that,
but then to do this

251
00:11:28,518 --> 00:11:30,061
don't you need to have
a gold nugget?

252
00:11:34,441 --> 00:11:35,984
I believe I saw
your move there, friend.

253
00:11:36,109 --> 00:11:37,986
- You did, did you?
- You won't mind?

254
00:11:38,111 --> 00:11:40,197
- Not at all. Not one little bit.
- Uh, now, come on.

255
00:11:40,363 --> 00:11:41,740
- What?
- You know I felt that.

256
00:11:41,907 --> 00:11:43,700
- Felt what?
- My wallet, please.

257
00:11:43,825 --> 00:11:44,910
I beg your pardon?

258
00:11:45,076 --> 00:11:47,537
- Sir.
- I-I mean, this?

259
00:11:48,038 --> 00:11:51,583
Oh, this. Is this it?
I've never seen that in my life.

260
00:11:51,750 --> 00:11:53,376
- So you're schooled in the craft?
- Yeah, I used to be.

261
00:11:53,501 --> 00:11:56,087
I'm a little rusty.
But you know what? Worth a shot.

262
00:11:58,298 --> 00:12:00,258
- Wait a minute! Where's my gold?
- What?

263
00:12:00,383 --> 00:12:01,718
I don't know
what you're talking about.

264
00:12:01,843 --> 00:12:03,595
Bro, that thing is real.

265
00:12:03,720 --> 00:12:05,847
All right, sir. We will get you
your gold back.

266
00:12:05,972 --> 00:12:07,224
- Jane.
- I don't have your gold.

267
00:12:07,390 --> 00:12:08,808
Maybe the kid
can enlighten you.

268
00:12:08,934 --> 00:12:10,101
The ki...

269
00:12:18,193 --> 00:12:20,779
Here we go, Lisbon...
Gossip.

270
00:12:23,698 --> 00:12:26,368
Two sarsaparillas, please.

271
00:12:26,993 --> 00:12:29,037
Sure I can't Irish those up for ya?

272
00:12:29,162 --> 00:12:32,123
Oh, would that I could,
but we're on the job.

273
00:12:33,667 --> 00:12:35,585
Are you guys here
about the murder?

274
00:12:35,752 --> 00:12:38,672
I mean, not that you stand out
in your suits or anything, but...

275
00:12:38,797 --> 00:12:40,048
We're with the CBI.

276
00:12:40,590 --> 00:12:42,759
Yes. Um, can I ask you...

277
00:12:43,468 --> 00:12:45,845
Do they let you use
your real name in this shop?

278
00:12:46,012 --> 00:12:49,015
I guess. Um, I'm Lily...
Soto.

279
00:12:49,182 --> 00:12:50,475
You looked a little spooked
just then.

280
00:12:50,642 --> 00:12:52,435
- Did you know Hollis?
- Barely.

281
00:12:52,644 --> 00:12:55,647
I grew up in Jasper...
in the valley.

282
00:12:55,855 --> 00:12:57,983
He interviewed me for this job
like a year ago.

283
00:12:58,108 --> 00:12:59,526
Did his employees like him?

284
00:12:59,693 --> 00:13:03,530
Yeah. Yeah, I mean,
he tips us out at Christmas.

285
00:13:03,697 --> 00:13:06,408
He's generous...
too much, probably.

286
00:13:06,992 --> 00:13:09,411
Well, I imagine that some people
bad-mouth him

287
00:13:09,536 --> 00:13:11,871
once the booze starts to flow.

288
00:13:11,997 --> 00:13:14,249
Oh. I mean, you know,
people talk.

289
00:13:14,582 --> 00:13:16,001
Some people weren't happy

290
00:13:16,126 --> 00:13:17,502
with the way he was
running the town.

291
00:13:17,627 --> 00:13:18,920
He kept this place going.

292
00:13:19,462 --> 00:13:22,632
Yeah, but it's... it's been getting
bad for a while.

293
00:13:22,841 --> 00:13:26,553
Rumor is there's a mall moving in.
Like, wow, right? But...

294
00:13:26,678 --> 00:13:28,722
People are freaking out
about these meth labs.

295
00:13:28,888 --> 00:13:30,098
Some friends were gonna
buy the bar

296
00:13:30,223 --> 00:13:31,725
but everybody's moving away.

297
00:13:32,183 --> 00:13:35,228
Francisco Navarro... was he here
last night at 8:00 p.m.?

298
00:13:35,353 --> 00:13:38,189
Yeah. Yeah. I mean, he's pretty much
here every night.

299
00:13:38,315 --> 00:13:39,816
You would be, too,
if you drank for free.

300
00:13:40,608 --> 00:13:41,901
Is that on?

301
00:13:43,862 --> 00:13:45,572
Probably not since disco.

302
00:13:48,658 --> 00:13:51,244
Hollis Percy overpaid
his workers.

303
00:13:51,369 --> 00:13:54,956
He hired three bartenders
when two would suffice.

304
00:13:55,165 --> 00:13:56,374
And yet he's dead.

305
00:13:56,541 --> 00:13:58,626
So, do you think
his generosity got him killed?

306
00:13:58,793 --> 00:14:01,296
I just think it's odd
that such a swell guy

307
00:14:01,463 --> 00:14:03,548
felt compelled to pay
for loyalty.

308
00:14:08,136 --> 00:14:10,430
So the police have no surveillance
footage on Main Street.

309
00:14:10,597 --> 00:14:11,639
Nope.

310
00:14:11,765 --> 00:14:14,309
And we follow up by driving
out to the woods. Why?

311
00:14:18,396 --> 00:14:19,689
Timing.

312
00:14:21,066 --> 00:14:23,485
An explosion the night
before a murder...

313
00:14:23,651 --> 00:14:25,403
doesn't that seem suspicious
to you?

314
00:14:27,530 --> 00:14:29,908
Not really. They're meth labs.
They explode.

315
00:14:30,158 --> 00:14:31,576
Well, let me ask you this.

316
00:14:31,826 --> 00:14:34,120
Doesn't something look
off here?

317
00:14:34,913 --> 00:14:36,122
Yeah, everything's burned.

318
00:14:36,247 --> 00:14:38,166
Yeah, but this is not
completely torched.

319
00:14:40,418 --> 00:14:42,045
- Feel that.
- I'm good.

320
00:14:42,212 --> 00:14:43,838
It feels like a flame-retardant gel.

321
00:14:44,798 --> 00:14:46,925
That's why some of this stuff
here survived.

322
00:14:47,133 --> 00:14:48,843
Before this place burned down
someone...

323
00:14:48,968 --> 00:14:50,095
someone sprayed it down

324
00:14:50,220 --> 00:14:52,097
so that the flames wouldn't
spread into a forest fire.

325
00:14:52,222 --> 00:14:53,848
- So this wasn't an accident?
- No.

326
00:14:54,307 --> 00:14:55,517
Okay.

327
00:14:56,309 --> 00:14:58,686
- Then, what was it?
- So you think it was arson?

328
00:14:58,853 --> 00:15:01,689
Well, maybe it was a rival gang trying
to make a point, like a territorial thing?

329
00:15:01,815 --> 00:15:04,943
Yeah, but what kinda drug dealer
sprays the place down for safety?

330
00:15:05,110 --> 00:15:06,319
I don't know.

331
00:15:06,486 --> 00:15:08,488
There might be a connection
to Hollis' murder here.

332
00:15:09,072 --> 00:15:10,657
It's weird.
It's almost as if someone

333
00:15:10,782 --> 00:15:12,700
mocked these places up
to make them look like meth labs.

334
00:15:12,867 --> 00:15:14,786
Okay, you and Cho look into it.

335
00:15:27,215 --> 00:15:29,175
Uh, put your toolboxes down, please.

336
00:15:31,636 --> 00:15:33,263
Here to fix the air conditioning.

337
00:15:33,888 --> 00:15:35,056
All right.

338
00:15:39,936 --> 00:15:42,063
We're at the door.
30 seconds till we're in.

339
00:16:15,094 --> 00:16:16,762
Go ahead. Say it.

340
00:16:17,555 --> 00:16:19,474
Then you're supposed
to say, "Say what?"

341
00:16:21,058 --> 00:16:25,021
And then I say, "You think I'm an idiot
for buying Van Pelt that orchid."

342
00:16:25,229 --> 00:16:27,315
You know what?
I'm glad I did.

343
00:16:27,482 --> 00:16:29,025
'Cause now the ball's
in her court.

344
00:16:29,525 --> 00:16:31,486
I'm gonna be so Zen
about this.

345
00:16:31,694 --> 00:16:33,529
Yep, the next move's hers.

346
00:16:33,738 --> 00:16:36,073
I'm just gonna be the reed
that bends in the breeze.

347
00:16:37,033 --> 00:16:38,075
You're still in love with her.

348
00:16:38,201 --> 00:16:39,994
But instead of telling her,
you bought her a plant.

349
00:16:42,330 --> 00:16:43,831
Yeah, that's pretty Zen, though.
Right?

350
00:16:43,956 --> 00:16:46,083
Hey, fellas.
We're gonna need this space.

351
00:16:47,960 --> 00:16:49,337
How long are you boys
gonna be fiddling

352
00:16:49,504 --> 00:16:51,088
with those new fangled
contraptions there?

353
00:16:51,255 --> 00:16:53,591
CBI arson unit, sir.
We'll be done shortly.

354
00:16:55,009 --> 00:16:57,303
Look, Mr. Navarro's kicking my butt...

355
00:16:57,428 --> 00:16:59,138
trying to get the show
back up here.

356
00:16:59,263 --> 00:17:00,806
We've been shut down
all day.

357
00:17:00,932 --> 00:17:04,310
Hi. I'm Ian Percy.
I'm in charge here.

358
00:17:04,435 --> 00:17:05,728
Are you gentlemen
with the CBI?

359
00:17:05,853 --> 00:17:08,564
Yes, Mr. Percy. I'm Agent Cho.
This is Agent Rigsby.

360
00:17:08,731 --> 00:17:10,441
- CBI arson unit.
- Arson unit?

361
00:17:10,608 --> 00:17:12,360
We're driving up
to check out those cabin fires.

362
00:17:12,527 --> 00:17:13,903
Hitting it bright and early
tomorrow morning.

363
00:17:14,028 --> 00:17:16,280
Oh, it's arson, you think?
Isn't that...

364
00:17:16,489 --> 00:17:19,033
wasn't it supposed to be
some crystal meth mishap?

365
00:17:19,158 --> 00:17:20,910
Well, fortunately
it didn't all burn down.

366
00:17:21,035 --> 00:17:22,411
Once we get the chemical
signature of the burn

367
00:17:22,537 --> 00:17:23,788
we can tell if someone
torched it.

368
00:17:23,913 --> 00:17:25,373
We'll find our bad guy
pretty quick after that.

369
00:17:25,498 --> 00:17:27,124
Let me know if you need
anything from me.

370
00:17:27,458 --> 00:17:29,210
In the meantime,
I'll let you get back to it.

371
00:17:49,188 --> 00:17:50,314
Hello, Chief.

372
00:17:50,481 --> 00:17:53,192
You got no case.
You're wasting your time.

373
00:17:54,527 --> 00:17:57,655
So, you saw me carrying
a gas can. Big deal.

374
00:17:58,322 --> 00:18:01,284
Besides, you didn't even take
your fancy equipment outta the truck.

375
00:18:01,409 --> 00:18:02,660
Yeah, there was no fancy equipment.

376
00:18:02,827 --> 00:18:04,412
Just your guilty conscience.

377
00:18:04,662 --> 00:18:05,997
Want something to drink?

378
00:18:06,247 --> 00:18:07,957
It's a long drive back
to Sacramento.

379
00:18:08,124 --> 00:18:09,750
You look cozy there, Chief.

380
00:18:09,875 --> 00:18:11,711
He knows how this goes.

381
00:18:11,877 --> 00:18:13,170
He's a cop.

382
00:18:13,296 --> 00:18:14,880
And you're a crook, too, right?

383
00:18:15,047 --> 00:18:17,550
That's why you built these phony
meth labs, burned 'em down?

384
00:18:17,675 --> 00:18:19,343
And you were trying
to scare people away.

385
00:18:20,136 --> 00:18:22,722
Drive down prices
and buy property on the cheap.

386
00:18:24,348 --> 00:18:25,433
Look...

387
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:27,810
even if I did set
a couple cabins on fire...

388
00:18:29,437 --> 00:18:30,855
So what?

389
00:18:31,439 --> 00:18:32,982
Maybe I like fires.

390
00:18:33,190 --> 00:18:34,734
Listen, Emmett...

391
00:18:35,359 --> 00:18:36,819
this isn't hypothetical.

392
00:18:36,944 --> 00:18:39,196
Right now, you're our prime suspect
in this murder case.

393
00:18:39,322 --> 00:18:40,406
You know what to do.

394
00:18:40,531 --> 00:18:42,491
You don't wanna be wrapped up
in a murder investigation.

395
00:18:42,700 --> 00:18:44,410
Help yourself out.

396
00:18:46,329 --> 00:18:49,081
All right, look, um, the thing
is in about three to four years

397
00:18:49,206 --> 00:18:50,666
a new outlet mall
is gonna open here...

398
00:18:50,791 --> 00:18:52,043
uh, next to Percy.

399
00:18:52,251 --> 00:18:55,004
So I figure I could
hold onto the land till then.

400
00:18:55,296 --> 00:18:58,549
- Then... dump it?
- But Hollis figured it out?

401
00:18:58,716 --> 00:19:00,551
- And he confronted you?
- No.

402
00:19:00,676 --> 00:19:02,511
- Hollis was clueless.
- So what did happen?

403
00:19:02,637 --> 00:19:04,305
My colleague here
thinks you killed him.

404
00:19:04,597 --> 00:19:06,349
So far I can't tell
if you have the stones.

405
00:19:06,474 --> 00:19:08,267
No... no...
No, guys...

406
00:19:09,185 --> 00:19:10,853
Guys, I'm...

407
00:19:11,979 --> 00:19:14,565
Hollis didn't suspect a thing,
I'm telling you.

408
00:19:15,441 --> 00:19:18,152
He was just some rich... weirdo.

409
00:19:18,319 --> 00:19:20,613
- They both are.
- They? Who's they? The son?

410
00:19:20,738 --> 00:19:22,615
Joanna... his wife.

411
00:19:23,032 --> 00:19:24,742
Uh, two weeks ago,
I get a call to the house...

412
00:19:24,909 --> 00:19:26,327
the neighbor heard
some screaming.

413
00:19:26,452 --> 00:19:27,745
He was drunk.

414
00:19:28,496 --> 00:19:30,164
And she was outta control...

415
00:19:31,415 --> 00:19:32,750
shrieking.

416
00:19:34,543 --> 00:19:36,796
Shrieking... about what?

417
00:19:45,596 --> 00:19:48,057
Jane made a lotta
Red John connections here.

418
00:19:48,182 --> 00:19:50,226
It looks like he's building
his own time line.

419
00:19:50,601 --> 00:19:53,312
It's all on the drive. We printed out
some greatest hits.

420
00:19:54,522 --> 00:19:56,691
- This is good.
- We could log it into the system.

421
00:19:56,816 --> 00:20:00,027
- Not necessary.
- So you'll just... take it from here?

422
00:20:00,444 --> 00:20:01,779
I will.

423
00:20:14,417 --> 00:20:16,544
He was sober for 20 years.

424
00:20:16,669 --> 00:20:20,172
A month ago, he fell off the wagon
and he could be a mean drunk.

425
00:20:20,297 --> 00:20:21,465
Did he hit you?

426
00:20:23,217 --> 00:20:24,593
- No.
- No?

427
00:20:26,137 --> 00:20:27,430
Okay.

428
00:20:28,764 --> 00:20:30,641
What could have caused him
to start drinking again?

429
00:20:30,766 --> 00:20:33,519
Well, he had his triggers.
But his father's been dead a long time.

430
00:20:33,644 --> 00:20:35,855
Were there romantic problems?

431
00:20:36,689 --> 00:20:38,023
You're sweet.

432
00:20:40,568 --> 00:20:43,195
You mean, was Hollis sleeping around?

433
00:20:43,362 --> 00:20:44,905
Or maybe you found out.

434
00:20:45,364 --> 00:20:48,826
Maybe you argued, and he was drunk
and things got outta control.

435
00:20:49,910 --> 00:20:51,871
Hollis used to run around.

436
00:20:52,329 --> 00:20:53,664
He was fun...

437
00:20:55,750 --> 00:20:57,168
too much fun.

438
00:20:58,169 --> 00:20:59,837
But he grew to be faithful.

439
00:21:01,005 --> 00:21:03,174
He was terrified of change.

440
00:21:04,759 --> 00:21:06,051
A wife knows.

441
00:21:07,261 --> 00:21:10,222
If you were married, you'd understand
what I was talking about.

442
00:21:13,058 --> 00:21:15,227
Are you married, Agent Van Pelt?

443
00:21:15,394 --> 00:21:17,605
Not yet. Sounds like fun.

444
00:21:18,481 --> 00:21:20,858
I like the police chief for the murder.
He's a weasel.

445
00:21:21,025 --> 00:21:22,234
Real estate records show
that Cook

446
00:21:22,359 --> 00:21:25,279
and some poorly rigged holding
company bought properties in town.

447
00:21:25,446 --> 00:21:27,448
He had a lot to lose
if Hollis exposed his scam.

448
00:21:27,615 --> 00:21:28,991
Juicy stuff.

449
00:21:29,283 --> 00:21:31,911
You have him pegged right as a weasel
but I don't think he's a killer.

450
00:21:32,036 --> 00:21:34,747
I think Joanna hides it well,
but Hollis may have been abusive.

451
00:21:34,872 --> 00:21:36,040
You're thinking self-defense.

452
00:21:36,165 --> 00:21:38,584
There is one vexing detail still missing.

453
00:21:38,709 --> 00:21:42,588
Something in Hollis' life changed...
a month ago.

454
00:21:42,713 --> 00:21:44,048
You mean, to get him drinking again.

455
00:21:44,215 --> 00:21:46,842
Yeah, exactly. Something.
We have to figure out what it was.

456
00:21:47,009 --> 00:21:48,093
Okay. How?

457
00:21:48,260 --> 00:21:51,680
Well, Joanna has no idea
or she has no inclination to tell us.

458
00:21:52,056 --> 00:21:53,265
Nor does anyone else.

459
00:21:53,432 --> 00:21:56,393
Maybe something
in his past re-emerged.

460
00:21:56,519 --> 00:21:58,562
- That's thin.
- Yeah. That is thin.

461
00:21:59,438 --> 00:22:00,898
But you have to start somewhere.

462
00:22:02,483 --> 00:22:04,193
Beautiful orchid, Grace.

463
00:22:04,485 --> 00:22:05,986
Well-chosen, Rigsby.

464
00:22:26,924 --> 00:22:29,969
Hey.
Set you up with another sarsaparilla?

465
00:22:30,678 --> 00:22:33,597
- On the house.
- Ah. Who'd say no to that?

466
00:22:33,848 --> 00:22:36,350
I'm gonna get going. Agent Jane.

467
00:22:37,184 --> 00:22:39,311
- See you around.
- Sure thing, Mr. Percy.

468
00:22:39,436 --> 00:22:41,272
Mr. Percy? Very formal.

469
00:22:41,397 --> 00:22:44,441
Uh, well, you know, it pays to kiss
the ring.

470
00:22:45,067 --> 00:22:47,236
So, you cracked your case yet?

471
00:22:48,320 --> 00:22:51,782
Cracked, uh, mm...
not likely. No.

472
00:22:51,907 --> 00:22:54,952
Uh, when does Francisco
usually show up here?

473
00:22:55,077 --> 00:22:56,453
He should be here soon.

474
00:22:57,413 --> 00:22:59,665
I heard you clowned Kevin pretty good.

475
00:22:59,999 --> 00:23:01,917
Kevin... the magician.

476
00:23:02,209 --> 00:23:05,337
Oh, him. Yes. Wouldn't call him
a magician.

477
00:23:05,504 --> 00:23:07,715
Normally has to be magic involved.

478
00:23:08,132 --> 00:23:09,174
Yeah.

479
00:23:09,300 --> 00:23:10,926
I hear you got real powers.

480
00:23:11,051 --> 00:23:14,013
No, I... just a little mind reading
and predictions, that's all.

481
00:23:14,138 --> 00:23:15,306
Show me.

482
00:23:15,723 --> 00:23:16,932
Please?

483
00:23:18,434 --> 00:23:19,602
Okay.

484
00:23:19,727 --> 00:23:22,479
How about I tell you what you're gonna
have for breakfast tomorrow morning?

485
00:23:22,646 --> 00:23:23,689
Okay.

486
00:23:23,814 --> 00:23:27,318
I'm gonna need a pen, paper,
and an envelope.

487
00:23:32,406 --> 00:23:33,532
Tasty.

488
00:23:34,158 --> 00:23:35,951
- Voila.
- Thank you.

489
00:23:36,118 --> 00:23:37,411
Close your eyes.

490
00:23:38,495 --> 00:23:41,624
I want you to imagine being
in your kitchen tomorrow morning.

491
00:23:41,790 --> 00:23:45,502
This is what I like about tending bar...
you meet all kinds.

492
00:23:45,669 --> 00:23:47,171
Visualize yourself...

493
00:23:48,672 --> 00:23:50,424
looking into the cupboard...

494
00:23:52,426 --> 00:23:56,096
looking at all the items thinking
about what you're gonna eat.

495
00:23:56,263 --> 00:23:58,515
And then you choose
which one you want.

496
00:23:59,141 --> 00:24:01,644
- You got it? You sure?
- Yeah.

497
00:24:03,896 --> 00:24:05,064
Okay.

498
00:24:10,486 --> 00:24:11,528
Done.

499
00:24:12,196 --> 00:24:14,406
Put it somewhere safe.
We'll open it tomorrow.

500
00:24:14,531 --> 00:24:16,450
- No peeking.
- Oh, I wouldn't dream of it.

501
00:24:17,910 --> 00:24:19,161
Barmaid!

502
00:24:19,370 --> 00:24:21,372
Shot of whiskey and a beer back.

503
00:24:32,800 --> 00:24:34,593
- What do you need?
- Backup.

504
00:24:34,718 --> 00:24:35,886
I don't like it when you say
you need backup

505
00:24:36,011 --> 00:24:37,346
and nothing's happened yet.

506
00:24:37,471 --> 00:24:39,223
Nothing to worry about.
We're just here to talk.

507
00:24:43,769 --> 00:24:45,354
Uh, do you mind?

508
00:24:48,524 --> 00:24:51,068
Oh, she didn't have to go anywhere.

509
00:24:53,028 --> 00:24:55,114
I already said my piece, but go ahead.

510
00:24:55,280 --> 00:24:57,199
Well, we're interested
in ancient history...

511
00:24:57,324 --> 00:24:58,617
which seems to be your department

512
00:24:58,784 --> 00:25:00,577
so it's obvious that we start
with you.

513
00:25:00,744 --> 00:25:03,330
We wanna know
about Hollis Percy's drinking.

514
00:25:03,497 --> 00:25:05,207
Well, he used to drink
and then he stopped.

515
00:25:05,416 --> 00:25:07,960
Then he started again.
You have any idea why?

516
00:25:08,168 --> 00:25:10,963
Well, who knows
what drives any other man?

517
00:25:11,088 --> 00:25:13,757
- Mr. Navarro, answer the question.
- Let's try this another way.

518
00:25:13,924 --> 00:25:15,551
Y-you and Hollis used
to knock around together...

519
00:25:15,676 --> 00:25:18,429
- you're lifelong buddies, huh?
- Yeah, we used to have some fun.

520
00:25:18,595 --> 00:25:20,347
Well, it seems there was nothing
in his current life

521
00:25:20,472 --> 00:25:22,391
that would drive him to drink,
so I am guessing

522
00:25:22,516 --> 00:25:25,769
it was something from his old life
that came outta the woodwork.

523
00:25:25,936 --> 00:25:29,815
Or someone. Tell me...
how far off am I?

524
00:25:34,403 --> 00:25:36,238
No idea what you're talking about.

525
00:25:37,072 --> 00:25:38,365
Really?

526
00:25:38,657 --> 00:25:40,534
You know what I find sad?

527
00:25:40,701 --> 00:25:43,078
The way you're so tucked
in the Percy family pocket.

528
00:25:43,287 --> 00:25:46,498
- Tucked in their pocket?
- Yeah. Like a soiled hankie.

529
00:25:48,542 --> 00:25:50,544
- You got a problem here, boss?
- No. No problem.

530
00:25:50,961 --> 00:25:54,214
No we're... we're fine.
Okay? We're good?

531
00:25:54,465 --> 00:25:56,258
You are hiding information
from the police, though

532
00:25:56,383 --> 00:25:57,468
which is a crime.

533
00:25:57,593 --> 00:26:00,179
- Why would I do that?
- Well, possibly to protect yourself

534
00:26:00,304 --> 00:26:02,723
by protecting Hollis Percy.
I'm not sure.

535
00:26:02,973 --> 00:26:05,017
- What's he talking about?
- He doesn't know.

536
00:26:05,142 --> 00:26:06,268
Yes, but you do.

537
00:26:06,477 --> 00:26:09,188
You talk a big game about fighting
for this town, fighting for the staff.

538
00:26:09,313 --> 00:26:11,899
But the truth is you sold out
to Hollis a long time ago.

539
00:26:12,149 --> 00:26:14,068
So long ago, you probably thought
he was your friend

540
00:26:14,193 --> 00:26:16,236
which is not sad... that's just stupid.

541
00:26:16,403 --> 00:26:17,780
You son of a bitch!

542
00:26:20,282 --> 00:26:21,825
Maybe we got a problem.

543
00:26:24,161 --> 00:26:26,121
- Is this part of the show?
- "Knock it off!"

544
00:26:26,246 --> 00:26:27,289
No.

545
00:26:38,932 --> 00:26:42,019
He coulda asked nicely,
that's all I was saying.

546
00:26:42,227 --> 00:26:43,562
I was there.

547
00:26:43,970 --> 00:26:46,598
- You want more coffee?
- No, I'm good.

548
00:26:47,557 --> 00:26:49,768
And Jane thinks you know
who came after Hollis Percy.

549
00:26:51,144 --> 00:26:52,813
Based on what, precisely?

550
00:26:52,938 --> 00:26:54,606
Well, we don't need to be precise.
You do.

551
00:26:54,815 --> 00:26:55,857
Okay.

552
00:26:57,109 --> 00:26:59,277
Yeah, we got into some trouble
when we were kids.

553
00:26:59,402 --> 00:27:01,988
Hollis got involved
with this one crazy girl...

554
00:27:02,155 --> 00:27:05,992
townie from a few miles up the road...
she was pretty.

555
00:27:07,828 --> 00:27:10,497
This was right after he married Joanna.

556
00:27:11,289 --> 00:27:12,415
He do that a lot?

557
00:27:12,582 --> 00:27:14,709
- Slumming it with the local girls?
- Yeah, he did.

558
00:27:14,876 --> 00:27:16,837
But this girl got pregnant.

559
00:27:17,003 --> 00:27:18,672
And Hollis paid her to...

560
00:27:19,297 --> 00:27:20,382
to deal with it.

561
00:27:21,675 --> 00:27:22,884
Her brother found out...

562
00:27:23,260 --> 00:27:24,719
uh, her brother was bad news.

563
00:27:24,845 --> 00:27:26,596
He roughed up Hollis
pretty thoroughly.

564
00:27:26,721 --> 00:27:28,140
And the brother held a grudge
this whole time.

565
00:27:28,265 --> 00:27:30,142
Yeah, he might have.
He was in prison for 12 years.

566
00:27:30,267 --> 00:27:31,643
He got out a month ago.

567
00:27:31,768 --> 00:27:33,895
I thought maybe he came looking
for a handout from Hollis.

568
00:27:34,062 --> 00:27:35,313
The rich guy he had dirt on.

569
00:27:35,480 --> 00:27:37,107
- Something like that.
- What happened to the girl?

570
00:27:37,232 --> 00:27:39,943
Well, we heard she went back home.
This was 30 years ago.

571
00:27:42,529 --> 00:27:43,655
Hollis was a mess.

572
00:27:44,906 --> 00:27:47,868
His dad found out...
almost disowned him.

573
00:27:48,952 --> 00:27:52,414
- Poor guy.
- Yes, we had a falling out, but...

574
00:27:54,207 --> 00:27:55,792
Hollis was always my friend.

575
00:27:55,917 --> 00:27:57,836
And Ian... he had no idea.
He's a good kid.

576
00:27:57,961 --> 00:28:00,463
And you may have given
your friend's killer a 2-day head start.

577
00:28:01,256 --> 00:28:03,592
I need to know the guy's name...
the brother.

578
00:28:05,594 --> 00:28:07,888
Flores. Dominic Flores.

579
00:28:56,561 --> 00:28:59,064
- You gonna touch all of 'em?
- Haven't touched that one.

580
00:28:59,898 --> 00:29:02,150
- I'm looking for poppy.
- Lisbon took it.

581
00:29:02,317 --> 00:29:03,652
If you want poppy,
grab the everything bagel.

582
00:29:03,818 --> 00:29:05,278
Yeah, but it has all that other stuff on it.

583
00:29:05,445 --> 00:29:07,572
Like those onion scraps, I mean,
what are the point of those?

584
00:29:07,781 --> 00:29:09,699
- All you see are problems.
- All I see are bagels.

585
00:29:09,824 --> 00:29:10,992
Just make a choice.

586
00:29:21,169 --> 00:29:22,212
Great.

587
00:29:27,884 --> 00:29:29,970
Where are we with the brother...
Hollis' old buddy?

588
00:29:30,095 --> 00:29:31,888
Oh, you mean Dominic Flores?
He's fresh outta Folsom

589
00:29:32,013 --> 00:29:34,516
serving 12 years and 7 weeks
for robbing... what did it say?

590
00:29:34,641 --> 00:29:36,476
16 jewelry stores and a pet shop.

591
00:29:36,601 --> 00:29:38,353
Yeah. He stole a boa constrictor.

592
00:29:38,520 --> 00:29:39,688
Of course he did.

593
00:29:39,813 --> 00:29:42,565
He was released a month ago.
His parole officer says he's been diligent.

594
00:29:42,732 --> 00:29:45,402
But the night of the murder
he was at a job fair in Sacramento.

595
00:29:45,527 --> 00:29:47,237
The coroner puts the attack
at 8:00 p.m.

596
00:29:47,362 --> 00:29:49,114
- He couldn't have been both places.
- I don't think so.

597
00:29:49,281 --> 00:29:51,449
I spoke to a guy who had
a booth at the job fair...

598
00:29:51,616 --> 00:29:52,909
a solar panel company.

599
00:29:53,076 --> 00:29:55,245
He said Dominic signed in
for an interview at 7:45.

600
00:29:55,370 --> 00:29:57,831
Okay. What about the sister, Juliana?

601
00:29:57,998 --> 00:30:00,667
Still looking. She bounced around for a bit
then fell out of touch with the family.

602
00:30:00,792 --> 00:30:04,212
So it's possible Dominic got out
and paid Hollis an awkward visit.

603
00:30:04,379 --> 00:30:06,840
That might explain his drinking
but it doesn't explain his murder.

604
00:30:07,007 --> 00:30:10,010
We should ask Joanna if she knew
about the pregnancy after the show.

605
00:30:10,427 --> 00:30:11,594
- What do you mean?
- Jane says

606
00:30:11,720 --> 00:30:14,055
we need to go
to the Medicine Man Show, obviously.

607
00:30:14,222 --> 00:30:16,099
Rigsby, you wanna drive?

608
00:30:16,266 --> 00:30:18,977
As long as I'm not called up
to volunteer, sure.

609
00:30:28,778 --> 00:30:31,364
- Ah, Kevin.
- You again.

610
00:30:31,573 --> 00:30:34,075
You know, I-I'm afraid
we got off on the wrong foot

611
00:30:34,200 --> 00:30:35,785
and you need my help,
I need your help.

612
00:30:35,910 --> 00:30:37,620
Maybe we can come to some
kind of agreement.

613
00:30:37,746 --> 00:30:40,415
- Why do I need your help?
- Your magic sucks eggs.

614
00:30:40,582 --> 00:30:43,001
I can help you with that,
and you can help me in return

615
00:30:43,126 --> 00:30:45,462
by giving me a couple of minutes
of your stage time.

616
00:30:45,628 --> 00:30:47,422
Sucks eggs, huh?

617
00:30:48,381 --> 00:30:51,009
- Pick a card. I won't look.
- All right.

618
00:30:53,845 --> 00:30:55,930
- Pick what card?
- You took my cards.

619
00:30:56,056 --> 00:30:57,307
I don't know what you're talking about.

620
00:30:57,432 --> 00:30:59,768
- Give me back my cards.
- Hey, hey, hey, Kevin...

621
00:30:59,893 --> 00:31:01,227
Kevin, let's modulate the attitude.

622
00:31:01,394 --> 00:31:04,064
- What's the problem?
- This guy is trying to get in my head.

623
00:31:04,230 --> 00:31:06,733
He's trying to ruin my act.
He stole my cards.

624
00:31:06,858 --> 00:31:09,319
Kevin, that's not true.
I-I think you should cool down

625
00:31:09,444 --> 00:31:10,904
before you go anywhere
near an audience.

626
00:31:11,029 --> 00:31:12,405
Stop playing with me.

627
00:31:12,530 --> 00:31:14,407
Mr. Jane, maybe you'd be better
off taking your seat.

628
00:31:14,532 --> 00:31:16,576
- I can handle this.
- Sure, I'd appreciate that.

629
00:31:16,701 --> 00:31:19,913
- He's a little scary.
- Oh, you're gonna handle it, Francisco?

630
00:31:20,080 --> 00:31:22,290
Handle this. I quit.

631
00:31:22,540 --> 00:31:23,708
Here you go.

632
00:31:23,917 --> 00:31:25,335
Candy-ass pickpocket tryin'
to bust my chops.

633
00:31:25,460 --> 00:31:27,504
- I beg your pardon.
- Nobody handles me.

634
00:31:27,629 --> 00:31:29,923
- What did you do?
- Who's he calling a candy-ass?

635
00:31:30,048 --> 00:31:32,342
Well, now I have no magic show.

636
00:31:32,467 --> 00:31:34,010
- Thank you. That's great...
- I know magic.

637
00:31:34,135 --> 00:31:36,346
- Yeah?
- Yes. What's your favorite number?

638
00:31:36,763 --> 00:31:38,890
- Uh, 23.
- Really?

639
00:31:39,224 --> 00:31:40,683
Check that pocket, there.

640
00:31:43,728 --> 00:31:45,814
- How'd you do that?
- Do I get the gig?

641
00:31:47,899 --> 00:31:48,942
Hell, yeah!

642
00:31:55,279 --> 00:31:57,990
- So what is it that Jane's gonna do?
- No idea.

643
00:31:58,866 --> 00:31:59,951
Really?

644
00:32:00,493 --> 00:32:03,913
Sometimes it's best just to be relaxed
about this stuff.

645
00:32:04,330 --> 00:32:06,457
Welcome, ladies and gentlemen

646
00:32:06,791 --> 00:32:08,793
and cowboys, too.

647
00:32:08,960 --> 00:32:11,546
Now, you all are in for a frontier treat

648
00:32:11,671 --> 00:32:14,006
that will mesmerize and amaze.

649
00:32:14,132 --> 00:32:16,843
I'm proud to present to you
the daring work

650
00:32:16,968 --> 00:32:21,055
of magician and mental mystic,
Patrick Jane!

651
00:32:21,180 --> 00:32:22,807
Let's hear it for him!

652
00:32:27,645 --> 00:32:29,063
Thank you, Doc Navarro.

653
00:32:29,730 --> 00:32:31,399
Very impressive introduction.

654
00:32:32,150 --> 00:32:34,152
I hope I don't disappoint.

655
00:32:34,360 --> 00:32:36,821
Some people say I have special powers.

656
00:32:37,196 --> 00:32:39,615
It's not true.
Everyone can read minds.

657
00:32:39,782 --> 00:32:42,994
I can just do it marginally better
than most, that's all.

658
00:32:43,119 --> 00:32:46,372
I'm gonna need some volunteers
for my first effect.

659
00:32:46,497 --> 00:32:49,167
I-I don't call them tricks
because I think tricks are mean

660
00:32:49,292 --> 00:32:51,502
and what we're here to do
is to have some fun.

661
00:32:51,711 --> 00:32:53,754
What about you two lovebirds
over there?

662
00:32:53,880 --> 00:32:55,465
- You here on your honeymoon?
- Uh, no.

663
00:32:55,631 --> 00:32:57,884
We're just... just passing through.
We got an R.V.

664
00:32:58,050 --> 00:32:59,844
Oh, great.
Don't tell me where you're from.

665
00:33:00,011 --> 00:33:02,972
But here, we're gonna start real simple.

666
00:33:03,347 --> 00:33:06,100
I'm gonna tell you the town
that you folks left from

667
00:33:06,225 --> 00:33:07,852
and the town that you folks
are headed to.

668
00:33:07,977 --> 00:33:10,313
Just write it down there
on that there piece of paper.

669
00:33:10,438 --> 00:33:12,607
And as you do it, imagine them.

670
00:33:12,982 --> 00:33:15,943
Imagine yourself pulling out
of the driveway.

671
00:33:16,569 --> 00:33:20,072
Lily Soto. You look like you should be
up here being my assistant.

672
00:33:20,198 --> 00:33:22,116
Ladies and gentlemen
Lily Soto.

673
00:33:27,163 --> 00:33:30,208
Okay, now I said that I would discover
the town that you folks are from

674
00:33:30,333 --> 00:33:32,001
and the town that you folks
are headed to.

675
00:33:33,586 --> 00:33:36,005
I believe you started out
in Albuquerque

676
00:33:37,215 --> 00:33:39,008
and you are headed to...

677
00:33:39,634 --> 00:33:41,385
to Seattle, Washington.

678
00:33:42,428 --> 00:33:43,846
How'd I do, Lily?

679
00:33:44,847 --> 00:33:46,974
"Albuquerque and Seattle."

680
00:33:49,101 --> 00:33:51,938
Thank you very much for your help.
You can take a seat. Lily...

681
00:33:52,438 --> 00:33:54,315
I'm not done with you yet.

682
00:33:54,524 --> 00:33:56,275
You have to stay up here and help me.

683
00:33:56,442 --> 00:33:59,237
I wrote down a prediction yesterday
and I gave it to you, yes?

684
00:33:59,403 --> 00:34:00,905
- You did.
- Yes.

685
00:34:01,030 --> 00:34:03,324
- Did you peek at it? Are you sure?
- No, I didn't peek at it.

686
00:34:03,449 --> 00:34:05,117
- I am positive.
- No little peeky-peeks?

687
00:34:05,243 --> 00:34:06,327
No peeks.

688
00:34:06,452 --> 00:34:08,079
And what did I say I'd predict?

689
00:34:08,204 --> 00:34:11,040
Uh, what I had for breakfast
this morning.

690
00:34:12,667 --> 00:34:14,585
Okay. That is impossible.

691
00:34:14,752 --> 00:34:18,839
Uh, and I have to admit...
I told a little white lie.

692
00:34:18,965 --> 00:34:21,008
That is not what I wrote down.

693
00:34:21,175 --> 00:34:22,969
In fact, what I wrote down...

694
00:34:23,761 --> 00:34:26,138
was the name of Hollis Percy's killer.

695
00:34:26,514 --> 00:34:28,558
That's right. Yes.

696
00:34:29,141 --> 00:34:30,768
I have an envelope, too.

697
00:34:31,269 --> 00:34:33,187
And inside this envelope...

698
00:34:33,813 --> 00:34:36,649
I wrote down the reason
for this brutal murder...

699
00:34:37,066 --> 00:34:39,318
the motive, if you will.

700
00:34:39,443 --> 00:34:43,155
Now, Lily, if you would please
open your envelope

701
00:34:43,406 --> 00:34:45,032
and read out that name.

702
00:34:45,157 --> 00:34:47,201
I... do I have to?

703
00:34:47,326 --> 00:34:48,786
I'm afraid you do.

704
00:34:53,291 --> 00:34:54,792
Ian Percy.

705
00:34:54,959 --> 00:34:57,044
Okay, Mr. Jane, that's quite enough.

706
00:34:57,169 --> 00:34:59,213
All right, this is insane.
Did you put him up to this?

707
00:34:59,338 --> 00:35:01,257
- Why would I do that?
- Why, indeed?

708
00:35:01,424 --> 00:35:04,468
Be-because he wants to get rid of me?
I don't know.

709
00:35:04,635 --> 00:35:05,678
He's trying to frame me.

710
00:35:05,845 --> 00:35:08,097
Well, if he's lying, then... then...

711
00:35:08,222 --> 00:35:11,017
you wouldn't object to Lily
opening this envelope

712
00:35:11,142 --> 00:35:12,643
and reading what's inside.

713
00:35:13,102 --> 00:35:15,313
Francisco, shut this travesty down.

714
00:35:15,438 --> 00:35:17,732
Uh, can't do that.
This is a CBI matter.

715
00:35:17,857 --> 00:35:20,359
- Right, Lisbon?
- Yes. I am afraid so.

716
00:35:20,484 --> 00:35:22,695
Ian, either Lily reads out aloud

717
00:35:22,820 --> 00:35:24,822
the reason you killed your father
for everyone to hear

718
00:35:24,947 --> 00:35:26,991
or you come with us...

719
00:35:27,116 --> 00:35:29,201
right now and confess.

720
00:35:29,785 --> 00:35:31,912
- The choice is yours.
- What's happening?

721
00:35:32,121 --> 00:35:35,374
I don't know. And... and I don't know
what you think happened

722
00:35:35,499 --> 00:35:38,002
but whatever's on that paper
it... it's not true.

723
00:35:38,127 --> 00:35:41,047
I think you do know.
And the clock is ticking.

724
00:35:42,256 --> 00:35:43,507
Are you ready, Lily?

725
00:35:44,967 --> 00:35:48,638
Ian, when Lily reads what's inside
I can't control what happens next.

726
00:35:50,181 --> 00:35:51,891
- Okay, I'll read it myself.
- No. No.

727
00:35:52,016 --> 00:35:55,227
Not... not now. Not... not here.

728
00:35:56,562 --> 00:35:57,813
I'm sorry, folks.

729
00:35:57,938 --> 00:36:00,024
I'll come with you, all right?
Please, look, whatever you want.

730
00:36:00,149 --> 00:36:01,233
Wise choice.

731
00:36:01,359 --> 00:36:03,611
Ian Percy, you're under arrest for murder.

732
00:36:03,736 --> 00:36:06,322
- Ian? Ian!
- It's... it's okay, Mom.

733
00:36:06,447 --> 00:36:07,782
It's gonna be okay.

734
00:36:24,799 --> 00:36:26,842
You were dating Lily, right?

735
00:36:27,510 --> 00:36:28,886
On the downlow?

736
00:36:33,349 --> 00:36:35,017
I thought Dad wouldn't approve.

737
00:36:36,394 --> 00:36:39,063
He thought the Percy name
was something special.

738
00:36:40,272 --> 00:36:41,816
And she wasn't like us.

739
00:36:42,650 --> 00:36:43,693
How'd you know?

740
00:36:43,818 --> 00:36:46,070
That you two are in love?
Oh, that was easy.

741
00:36:46,696 --> 00:36:47,822
Then there were little things...

742
00:36:47,947 --> 00:36:50,324
little things that said
that there was something, uh...

743
00:36:52,743 --> 00:36:53,869
off.

744
00:36:56,539 --> 00:36:59,917
You know, everybody thought
my dad was some good guy.

745
00:37:00,793 --> 00:37:02,753
And that he kept the town going,
you know?

746
00:37:02,878 --> 00:37:04,213
But they didn't know...

747
00:37:05,339 --> 00:37:07,341
how he terrorized us, that...

748
00:37:08,634 --> 00:37:10,302
He wasn't fit...

749
00:37:11,637 --> 00:37:13,013
not to raise a family.

750
00:37:15,307 --> 00:37:17,351
We need to know how it happened.

751
00:37:17,518 --> 00:37:19,145
Start a month ago?

752
00:37:20,730 --> 00:37:22,481
Oh, it started before that.

753
00:37:25,901 --> 00:37:27,653
When I first moved back
from San Francisco

754
00:37:27,778 --> 00:37:29,363
"it was like a few months ago."

755
00:37:32,074 --> 00:37:33,868
"Meeting Lily was..."

756
00:37:35,327 --> 00:37:36,996
"It was like a dream."

757
00:37:38,205 --> 00:37:39,582
And I figured he...

758
00:37:39,707 --> 00:37:42,168
he knew something
when he started drinking again.

759
00:37:43,294 --> 00:37:45,212
He was too ashamed to come
to me, so...

760
00:37:46,255 --> 00:37:47,840
I went to him.

761
00:37:48,257 --> 00:37:50,050
And he was drunk.

762
00:37:52,386 --> 00:37:55,389
I didn't know about his affair.
I mean, how could I have even guessed?

763
00:37:55,514 --> 00:37:57,266
"I'm trying to tell you..."

764
00:37:58,476 --> 00:38:00,352
"Lily is your sister."

765
00:38:01,103 --> 00:38:02,772
"No. No, that is absurd."

766
00:38:02,897 --> 00:38:04,940
- "No. No, she can't... no!"
- "I know my own daughter."

767
00:38:05,107 --> 00:38:06,901
"We didn't think
you were coming back."

768
00:38:08,068 --> 00:38:10,321
- "You abandoned us."
- "Mom knows?"

769
00:38:10,488 --> 00:38:12,031
"No, of course not."

770
00:38:12,656 --> 00:38:15,785
"Lily's mother and me are kind of a..."

771
00:38:16,535 --> 00:38:19,789
"a fling! You know, we were young..."

772
00:38:19,914 --> 00:38:21,707
"30 years ago."

773
00:38:23,125 --> 00:38:25,836
- "How could you do this to me?"
- "Do this to you?"

774
00:38:27,630 --> 00:38:29,757
"You spoiled brat."

775
00:38:29,924 --> 00:38:31,926
"What the hell did I ever do to you?"

776
00:38:32,092 --> 00:38:35,221
"I betrayed your mother not you
and that's my business."

777
00:38:36,472 --> 00:38:37,848
"Lily and you..."

778
00:38:38,057 --> 00:38:41,435
"That's on you.
You should've been able to tell."

779
00:38:41,602 --> 00:38:43,062
"So it's my fault, huh?"

780
00:38:43,187 --> 00:38:47,358
"It's not mine. Either way...
It doesn't matter."

781
00:38:47,483 --> 00:38:50,444
"So, son, you gotta suck it up
and move on!"

782
00:38:51,821 --> 00:38:53,155
"Lily is not for you."

783
00:38:53,280 --> 00:38:54,990
"She's the only girl I've ever loved!"

784
00:38:55,658 --> 00:38:57,117
"I wanna marry her!"

785
00:38:59,328 --> 00:39:01,664
"For the first time in your life, son"

786
00:39:01,831 --> 00:39:04,500
"face the facts that are staring you
in the face"

787
00:39:04,625 --> 00:39:05,960
"and stop whining!"

788
00:39:16,554 --> 00:39:18,264
What do I even say to her?

789
00:39:20,766 --> 00:39:22,768
"Dad had an affair."
Um...

790
00:39:23,227 --> 00:39:25,020
"I'm your brother."

791
00:39:25,771 --> 00:39:27,606
It's not gonna be easy.

792
00:39:28,691 --> 00:39:31,193
But you are gonna have to tell her
the truth at some point.

793
00:39:34,572 --> 00:39:36,448
I finally got through to him, you know?

794
00:39:38,951 --> 00:39:41,579
It felt good... for a second

795
00:39:42,538 --> 00:39:46,083
that the old bastard finally understood.

796
00:39:48,586 --> 00:39:50,170
When he was dying, he...

797
00:39:50,546 --> 00:39:52,840
finally understood
what he'd done to all of us.

798
00:39:57,595 --> 00:39:58,971
- Hey.
- Hey.

799
00:39:59,096 --> 00:40:01,765
You fooled a guy
with a mobile made-up arson lab?

800
00:40:01,932 --> 00:40:04,643
- Neat trick.
- He didn't seem to think so, but thanks.

801
00:40:05,769 --> 00:40:08,480
- So... good week then?
- Yeah. Yeah.

802
00:40:08,606 --> 00:40:09,982
Great, yeah.

803
00:40:10,149 --> 00:40:12,735
Just moving with the wind.

804
00:40:15,487 --> 00:40:17,323
No, I'm sorry. That was a lie.

805
00:40:17,531 --> 00:40:19,783
Uh, today is not a good day.

806
00:40:20,534 --> 00:40:23,162
- We have to talk.
- Okay. About what?

807
00:40:23,329 --> 00:40:25,247
- About you and me.
- Great.

808
00:40:25,372 --> 00:40:28,083
Because there's something I really
have been meaning to tell you, um...

809
00:40:28,667 --> 00:40:29,710
I...

810
00:40:30,044 --> 00:40:31,879
- Excuse me.
- Oh, hi!

811
00:40:33,088 --> 00:40:36,175
- I'm sorry I'm late. Traffic was just...
- It's fine.

812
00:40:37,635 --> 00:40:41,013
Oh, uh, uh, Duncan, this is Wayne.
Wayne, Duncan.

813
00:40:41,138 --> 00:40:43,766
- Wayne Rigsby. It's good to meet you.
- Pleasure.

814
00:40:43,933 --> 00:40:46,852
Wayne and I met in Los Angeles
at the White Hat program.

815
00:40:47,102 --> 00:40:49,313
Actually that's... that's where we met.

816
00:40:49,939 --> 00:40:52,358
- What did I say?
- Y-you said, "Wayne".

817
00:40:53,359 --> 00:40:56,153
Oh, s... I'm sorry.
Weird...

818
00:40:56,654 --> 00:41:00,282
Oh! I just remembered
I left something at my desk.

819
00:41:00,824 --> 00:41:03,285
Duncan, it's great to meet you.

